---

<CaseStudy
  entry={entry}
  standfirst="A tethered inspection robot for the inside of a fusion reactor, designed and operated in simulation before it existed. I owned the Isaac Sim program, which designed the robot, simulated its missions and left the sponsor a reusable reactor environment, and the ROS 2 stack and arm firmware that run it: mission logic, drive control, arm motion, perception, state estimation, the synthetic test rig and the CI that holds it together. This page is about the software and the problems that shaped it."
>
  <h2>The problem</h2>

  <p>
    DIII-D is a fusion research tokamak at General Atomics. The sponsor
    wanted a tethered robot that could enter through a port, orbit the
    graphite floor, and carry an arm for camera inspection and small
    manipulation, with all compute on the robot and everything down one
    tether. The floor is low-friction and uncertain, nothing inside may be
    touched or modified, and there was no budget for physical prototypes.
    Every drivetrain and arm decision therefore had to be made in
    simulation against the real reactor geometry and be right the first
    time, and the software had to be testable without the robot, because
    the robot did not exist for most of the term.
  </p>

  <Figure id="fortis-tokamak-context" eager />

  <DataTable {...capabilityTiers} />

  <h2>What I owned</h2>

  <p>
    I was the simulation and software lead on a five-person capstone team
    and the technical lead overall, from November 2025 to June 2026. Mine
    were the Isaac Sim program end to end: the reactor environment, the
    robot models, the design studies that chose the drivetrain and sized the
    arm, and the mission simulations that drove the robot through entry,
    orbit and arm work before any of it was built. Mine too were the ROS 2
    workspace and its containers and CI, the mission state machine, the
    drive control and kinematics, the arm firmware on the Teensy and the
    motion path above it, the perception and state estimation stack, the
    synthetic camera rig, the operator interface layouts, and the Jetson
    bring-up. I also turned simulation results into requirements for the
    mechanical work and ran the sprint reviews that gated design decisions.
    The chassis and arm mechanicals and the power system were teammates'
    work.
  </p>

  <DataTable {...architecture} />

  <p>
    Eleven ROS 2 packages plus the simulation tooling, roughly fifteen
    thousand lines of Python, 45 test files and 233 test functions, built
    and tested on every push by CI. The rest of this page walks through the
    problems in the order they were hit.
  </p>

  <h2>Simulation</h2>

  <h3>Free-body diagrams were answering the wrong question</h3>

  <p>
    The program started the way motor sizing usually does, with free-body
    analysis around a tank-tread chassis. The first physics test I ran in
    Isaac Sim invalidated it: a silicone tread on the 45 degree graphite
    slope could not hold traction, because silicone-on-graphite friction is
    too low to resist sliding at that grade. Friction, not torque, was the
    binding constraint, and the analysis had assumed the one thing it
    needed to prove. From then on nothing was procured until it had been
    simulated against the reactor mesh. Eight weeks of tread design were
    discarded, in simulation rather than after a purchase, and the slope
    was declared untraversable, which fixed the mission to the two lower
    floors.
  </p>

  <Figure id="fortis-validation-loop" />

  <h3>A reactor model no physics engine could collide against</h3>

  <p>
    The sponsor's model was a tile-resolved SolidWorks assembly on the
    order of 400 GB. MuJoCo was the first simulator and crashed during
    contact resolution on the dense geometry even after reduction. I moved
    the program to Isaac Sim on PhysX 5 and built a reduction pipeline: a
    smoothed STEP export replaced per-tile geometry with continuous
    surfaces, the upper vessel half and outer shell that the robot never
    touches were removed, the slope became a parametric ramp for solver
    stability, and the open edges at the step were closed because they
    produced collision glitches. Collision was then authored by hand in
    the Isaac Sim GUI, and the result is committed as an artifact that is
    never regenerated.
  </p>

  <p>
    Three engine constraints shaped every script after that. The Isaac Sim
    world singleton cannot be torn down and recreated in one process, so
    every parametric sweep runs one subprocess per test point and
    aggregates results outside. Triangle-mesh collision is static-only, so
    every dynamic body uses signed-distance-field, convex-hull or
    convex-decomposition collision instead. And the CAD could not be
    imported for physics, because gearbox internals form closed kinematic
    loops the articulation solver rejects, so every dynamic body was
    rebuilt from primitives with real masses.
  </p>

  <h3>The physics engine cannot model an omni wheel</h3>

  <p>
    Everything depended on simulating a holonomic drive on a slippery
    floor, and PhysX has no anisotropic friction. An omni wheel grips along
    its drive direction and rolls freely across it on passive rollers, and
    there is no way to declare a contact material that behaves differently
    along two axes. This took three iterations to get right.
  </p>

  <p>
    The first model approximated each wheel as rings of spheres, 72 per
    wheel, which drove but carried more friction than a real roller. The
    second imported the real wheel meshes and built each roller as its own
    rigid body on a free revolute joint, a 44 degree-of-freedom
    articulation per robot, with a convex hull on every roller barrel so
    the anisotropy came from geometry rather than a material property. That
    was physically right and numerically wrong: under load a single-sphere
    roller collider sank through the wheel hub, and a seven-sphere version
    over-torqued. The canonical model became a five-sphere chain per roller,
    with deliberate gaps between the spheres, because a continuous ring of
    spheres collides isotropically and degenerates back into a cylinder,
    erasing the very freedom the model exists to capture.
  </p>

  <p>
    I validated the five-sphere model against the single-sphere baseline
    with a full orbit sweep and kept the bias on the record: sphere chains
    resist more than free-spinning rollers, roughly 20 percent more torque
    on matched runs, so every motor margin the simulation reported is
    conservative by construction. A model biased the other way would have
    been useless for sizing motors.
  </p>

  <Figure id="fortis-isaac-viewport" />

  <h3>The skid-steer chassis could not turn on the step</h3>

  <p>
    After treads, the team moved to skid-steer. To orbit from its landing
    orientation the robot has to point-turn while straddling a 4.5 inch
    step. I wrote a parametric robot builder and swept 60 geometrically
    valid configurations of wheel radius, width, wheelbase, track and
    torque. Three completed the turn, all at 85 to 116 degrees of tilt with
    7 to 10 inches of radial drift, because every wheel arc crosses the step
    circle mid-turn and the wheels must climb a wall nearly their own
    radius. On the flat floor the differential wheel speed could not even
    generate the shear to rotate. That study, run with a friction
    coefficient more generous than the real floor, moved the program to an
    X-drive on omni wheels, which strafes the orbit with no yaw and never
    crosses the step.
  </p>

  <DataTable {...skidSteer} />

  <h3>Measuring torque honestly</h3>

  <p>
    The orbit sweep that sized the drive motors commands wheel velocities
    from a single inverse kinematics call after a settle and never corrects
    them, so the chassis drifts radially and tracking sits at 18 to 24
    percent by design. An open-loop command yields an honest torque
    measurement; a closed-loop tracker fighting position error would
    inflate the reading. The same sweep exposed the radius drift as a
    controls problem: the yaw rate was computed for the configured radius
    rather than the measured one, so the robot spiralled inward. That
    diagnosis is what the orbit generator in the drive stack was later
    written around.
  </p>

  <DataTable {...orbitResults} />

  <Figure id="fortis-orbit-torque" />

  <Clip id="orbitSim" />

  <h3>A joint space that is mostly invalid</h3>

  <p>
    Sizing the arm actuators meant sweeping its joint space, and most of
    that space puts the arm through the chassis, the floor or the vessel
    wall. A torque statistic over those poses describes a robot that cannot
    exist. I built a three-stage pipeline: a headless Monte Carlo sweep of
    5,000 poses drawn uniformly over all four joints at a fixed seed, with
    the payload applied in every sample; an analytical post-filter for
    chassis, self and floor collision, the reactor envelope and tip-over;
    and a plotting stage. The base yaw joint is sampled rather than fixed,
    because stability depends on it, and the sampler's uniformity is
    checked before anything is trusted, since pass rates over a biased
    draw are not pass rates.
  </p>

  <p>
    The filter never drops unstable poses. Every pose carries an analytic
    tip flag from projecting the center of gravity against the support
    polygon and a physics tip flag from the settle in simulation, plus a
    disagreement flag between them, so the two checks audit each other. The
    physics runs at the lightest plausible chassis mass and the margins are
    reported at the realistic one, an intentional asymmetry that makes every
    stability conclusion conservative. The filtered set is the shared input
    to both the joint torque and the chassis stability analyses, so
    actuator sizing and tip-over margin describe the same robot.
  </p>

  <DataTable {...armResults} />

  <FigurePair ids={['fortis-mc-sampling', 'fortis-poloidal-map']} />

  <DataTable {...actuators} />

  <p>
    The 25 poses that tip in physics but not analytically are dynamic
    rocking on the step, a mode only the physics check can see. The same
    sweep is what cut the sponsor's 50 inch reach to 30: at 50 the
    shoulder's demand passed any gearbox the project could source, and at 30
    every joint came inside margin.
  </p>

  <Figure id="fortis-arm-torque" />

  <Clip id="armSim" />

  <h2>The ROS 2 stack</h2>

  <h3>Infrastructure before nodes</h3>

  <p>
    Five people on five machines needed one software target, and the stack
    had to deploy to a Jetson. The effort began with infrastructure. Two
    containers share one ROS 2 Humble source tree: a CPU image that is the
    default for everyone and builds on x86 and ARM, and a GPU overlay on
    the Isaac ROS base that adds cuVSLAM, nvblox, cuMotion with its MoveIt 2
    adapter and GPU image processing for the machines that can use them.
    Both carry MoveIt 2, ros2_control, robot_localization and a Foxglove
    bridge. A small shell wrapper turns bring-up, branch switching and
    exec-into-container into one-line commands, so flipping the Jetson to a
    branch is a single operation.
  </p>

  <p>
    Lint runs on every commit through pre-commit and again in CI, and CI
    builds and tests the whole workspace on every pull request. Two
    isolation rules keep those tests honest: every package pins its own
    ROS domain id in a test fixture so parallel test processes cannot
    cross-talk over DDS, and CI runs packages sequentially as a second
    guard. Upstream hardware packages are vendored by a script from a pinned
    manifest rather than copied in.
  </p>

  <h3>Motion is state-gated</h3>

  <p>
    Inside a vessel that must not be touched, a drive or arm command
    arriving at the wrong moment is the worst failure available. The
    mission state machine is pure Python with no ROS or hardware
    dependency: nine states and fifteen events, with every transition and
    its guard expressed as a row in a table rather than nested
    conditionals. Guards read a context dictionary the caller supplies, so
    the same step with a different context is deterministic and the whole
    table was exercised exhaustively before it touched a robot. The machine
    can export itself as a diagram, a thin node bridges it to one topic per
    event and one per guard flag, and a terminal console drives it by hand
    during bring-up.
  </p>

  <Figure id="fortis-mission-states" />

  <p>
    Every action component sits above that machine rather than beside it.
    The drive accepts velocity commands only in the orbit and return-home
    states, and anything else, including the window before any state has
    arrived, is rejected. A rejected command publishes explicit zeros on
    both the internal wheel topic and the controller's command topic,
    because a safety brake should never have to infer a stop from the
    absence of a message. Two more mechanisms stop the wheels independently
    of the operator's client: entering any non-driving state commands zeros
    at once, and a dead-man watchdog commands zeros if no velocity arrives
    within half a second, since teleop clients stop publishing on release
    without sending a zero and the velocity controller would otherwise hold
    its last setpoint. Arming is a separate lock: a UI node wraps the
    controller manager's switch service behind a plain Bool topic and
    reports the armed state from the service result, not the request.
  </p>

  <p>
    Faults are edge-triggered and sticky. A status bridge folds the motor
    controllers' latched disarm reason into the active error field, because
    the firmware clears its transient error bits the moment a condition
    passes and a faulted axis would otherwise read clean. The bridge stops
    publishing entirely when any axis goes silent, so the health monitor's
    own watchdog trips rather than trusting a half-stale snapshot. The
    monitor starts with the drive reported unhealthy, flips true only after
    a clean snapshot, and emits the fault event on the true-to-false edge,
    which drives the mission into fault from any state through a wildcard
    transition. Leaving fault requires an explicit operator acknowledgement.
  </p>

  <Figure id="fortis-software-layers" />

  <h3>Drive control</h3>

  <p>
    The X-drive kinematics are a 4 by 3 matrix from body twist to wheel
    speeds with uniform saturation: a command that would exceed one wheel's
    limit is scaled as a whole so the commanded direction survives, and the
    pseudoinverse recovers the body twist from wheel feedback. The matrix
    stays ideal; the two hardware corrections, mirrored right-side motors
    and the camera-forward convention, live at the boundary in the drive
    node where they are documented on their constants. Wheel setpoints go
    through ros2_control to a joint-group velocity controller bound to the
    motor controllers over CAN, with a mock hardware plugin selectable from
    the URDF so CI and desk runs exercise the same controller
    configuration without a bus. Xacro tests expand every combination of
    those arguments and check the node ids, the joint lists and the URDF
    itself, since a swapped wheel on the bus would be nearly impossible to
    diagnose later.
  </p>

  <p>
    Three constants tie the kinematics to the robot description, and a
    regression test parses the xacro and fails the moment either side
    moves. Beside it sit two calibration tools: an off-robot UMBmark
    calculator that turns measured straight-line and rotation errors into
    corrected effective wheel radius and lever arm, and a velocity-loop
    autotuner that steps each motor controller through a coarse-to-fine
    gain grid on the ground, under load, scoring rise time, overshoot,
    settling and oscillation, because gains tuned off the ground do not
    transfer to a loaded robot.
  </p>

  <p>
    Two helpers sit on top of the command path. A heading hold arbitrates
    the operator's yaw command against a PID on the estimated yaw: while
    the operator is turning, their command passes through and the target
    tracks the live heading, and on release the controller holds the
    heading they stopped at, with stamp-driven timing and anti-windup. It
    works only because the estimate's yaw is gyroscope-owned, so the hold
    never chases wheel slip. An orbit generator turns a held button into a
    constant body twist of tangential strafe plus coupled yaw, with yaw rate
    equal to speed over radius, so the cameras keep facing the center; for
    a holonomic base that is a constant twist and needs no planner. Its own
    shorter dead-man makes release-to-stop crisp.
  </p>

  <Figure id="fortis-block-diagram" />

  <Clip id="controlDemo" />

  <h3>A robot description built for MoveIt 2 and ros2_control</h3>

  <p>
    The CAD tool's native URDF export was unusable for planning: closed
    kinematic loops with orphaned loop-closure links, no collision
    geometry, no joint limits, continuous joints where revolute was
    required, spurious prismatic constraints, and asymmetric gripper drive
    paths. I wrote the description by hand as xacro from the CAD treated as
    truth: 26 links and 25 joints as a clean tree of primitives with
    inertia macros, all dimensions in one constants file, revolute arm
    joints with limits, effort and velocity, a driven gripper finger with a
    mimic joint for the other, optical frames following the ROS camera
    convention so the depth driver plugs in without remapping, and a tool
    center point frame placed for grasp planning. The ros2_control block is
    included behind an argument, so the RViz-only workflow and the
    real-motor workflow share one file.
  </p>

  <h3>Arm firmware: deterministic timing and a fail-safe link</h3>

  <p>
    Step-and-direction control of a stepper needs pulse edges placed with
    microsecond determinism, and a general-purpose operating system can
    preempt a process for milliseconds, which on a step train is the
    difference between smooth motion and a stalled joint. So the arm's
    motion executor is firmware on a Teensy 4.1: a control state machine
    over boot, disabled, enabled, moving and fault, with a command-dispatch
    loop servicing the serial link between motion updates, coordinated
    multi-axis stepping for the three closed-loop stepper joints from
    hardware timers, and hardware PWM for the wrist and gripper servos. The
    stepping library first chosen would not compile against the current
    Teensy 4 core, so its Teensy 4 successor was vendored into the project
    and the firmware adapted to it.
  </p>

  <p>
    The link is safety-relevant and conductor-limited through the arm, so
    it is a custom binary protocol rather than text or middleware over
    serial: fixed-layout frames at 1 Mbaud with a start byte, length,
    sequence counter, type, payload, CRC-16-CCITT over sequence, type and
    payload, and an end byte, with commands for enable, disable, joint
    targets, joint velocities, home and clear-faults, acknowledge and
    negative-acknowledge responses with error codes, and asynchronous boot
    and fault events. A 250 ms heartbeat watchdog disables the motors if
    host traffic stops. Homing is teach-and-persist: the operator jogs the
    arm to its home pose, one command declares it the zero and marks the
    joints homed, and the reference is checkpointed to EEPROM in ping-pong
    slots with per-slot CRC, so the arm boots already homed and a power
    cycle recovers a valid position rather than an arbitrary one. The
    cycloidal joints are near self-locking unpowered, which is what makes a
    saved zero trustworthy. Safety is layered: motion is gated on drivers
    enabled and joints homed, faults latch until explicitly cleared, an
    external emergency-stop input is honored, and the heartbeat is the
    comms-loss interlock.
  </p>

  <p>
    The protocol was exercised first entirely in software against a
    pseudo-terminal mock of the firmware, then a host client drove the real
    board over USB, then the signal chain was verified at the level-shifter
    outputs before any driver was connected. One bring-up episode is worth
    recording: two joints held torque but would not step despite clean
    pulses on the oscilloscope. Tracing from the controller pin outward
    found two independent faults, one driver's control-signal selector left
    at the wrong logic level and one loose level-shifter jumper that scoped
    correctly on a high-impedance probe but carried no load. Verifying
    signal levels before connecting drivers is what kept those from becoming
    smoked hardware.
  </p>

  <h3>Arm motion and the MoveToPose contract</h3>

  <p>
    The arm interface was defined before its kinematics existed, because
    targeting and the operator interface needed a stable thing to call. A
    MoveToPose action carries a target pose in, progress out, and a
    success flag with a message back. The gripper is two Trigger services,
    because two endpoints map onto two operator buttons and two mission
    events. Both are gated on mission state with the same latched topic and
    the same throttled rejection pattern the drive uses, so reading one
    node tells you how the other works.
  </p>

  <p>
    Behind that contract is an analytic solver for the four-joint arm. Base
    yaw decouples from the rest, and with the URDF's pitch axes the
    shoulder, elbow and wrist reduce to a planar two-link chain in reach
    and down-positive height with exactly two elbow branches. For a target
    with no requested tool pitch the solver scans candidate pitches
    nearest-level first and returns the first pitch that yields a
    limit-respecting solution, so results are deterministic. A test drives
    81 in-limit joint configurations through forward kinematics and back
    and requires every one to close within a millimeter. The motion node
    solves every published target to drive the latched ik-ok flag that
    guards the transition from targeting to arm-at-view, reduces goal
    poses through TF into the arm base frame, streams joint waypoints at a
    configured joint speed with action feedback, and succeeds once the
    joint states settle inside tolerance or aborts on timeout. Goals are
    accepted only in the targeting, view, inspect and pick states; large
    repositions while carrying a payload are a mission decision, not an
    operator shortcut.
  </p>

  <p>
    MoveIt 2 ships in both containers, with the cuMotion planner and its
    MoveIt adapter staged in the GPU image, and the description above was
    written so the setup assistant can generate a config from a clean chain.
    The executor that ran on the robot is the analytic path, and because it
    sits behind the same action, a MoveIt planner replaces it without any
    caller changing.
  </p>

  <p>
    The serial bridge to the arm firmware is a background reader thread
    that reconnects forever, a heartbeat timer under 100 ms and a status
    poll, with status frames republished as joint states calibrated to
    radians and meters, a latched arm status message and diagnostics with
    decoded fault names. Firmware faults are forwarded as mission fault
    events. The frame codec is pure standard library and mirrors the
    firmware's framing exactly, so the bridge, the mock and the tests share
    one implementation, and the pseudo-terminal mock lets the bridge, the
    motion node and the full mission test run with no board attached.
  </p>

  <Figure id="fortis-arm-subsystem" />

  <h3>Cameras that could not stream where the code was written</h3>

  <p>
    The depth cameras cannot stream inside the development container, so
    the perception stack would have been untestable anywhere but the robot.
    I built a synthetic camera rig that publishes the exact topic contract
    of the real driver, compressed RGB, aligned depth, camera info and IMU,
    for one to four mounts at the URDF poses, over a procedural scene of
    planes, boxes and spheres rendered by a vectorized raycaster, with
    closed-form orbit, line and hold trajectories that give exact ground
    truth beside every frame. Two scene variants differ by exactly one box
    added and one removed, both of known volume, so the map-diff tests
    assert exact numbers. Injectable gyro bias and noise exercise the
    debiasing path.
  </p>

  <p>
    The crux was texture. Point-sampled speckle aliases once a pixel's
    footprint on a surface exceeds the noise cell, so the same wall rendered
    a different pattern from every pose, and that view-dependent shimmer
    corrupted feature matching. Visual odometry looked broken when the bug
    was the texture. The fix passes each hit's per-pixel footprint into the
    shader and fades every speckle octave out as the footprint grows, a
    small mip-map, with two octaves so surfaces stay trackable up close and
    across the room. Because checker and speckle are functions of world
    position only, a surface point renders the same color from any pose,
    which is exactly what cross-frame tracking needs.
  </p>

  <h3>Noticing what changed inside the vessel</h3>

  <p>
    A topic registry is the single source of truth for the perception
    chain. Each camera's aligned depth is back-projected into a colored
    cloud in its optical frame, dropping rather than queuing a pair that
    arrives while the previous one is still reprojecting. A fusion node
    buffers the newest cloud per camera, transforms all of them into the
    body frame on a fixed-rate timer, skips any camera whose transform is
    missing rather than stalling the rest, and voxel-downsamples the
    merge. A sparse voxel grid with per-voxel hit counts and first-seen
    color integrates the fused cloud in the odom frame; a map saved on one
    run becomes the reference for the next, and the diff is published as
    green-added and red-removed markers for the 3D panel and as a
    machine-readable summary of counts and volumes. For an inspection
    robot, that diff is the product.
  </p>

  <p>
    Detection runs behind one interface with two backends: an HSV blob
    detector for CI and the synthetic scene, and YOLOv8 through ONNX with
    weights fetched by a pinned, checksum-verified download. Boxes are
    lifted to 3D through the depth median and into the body frame, and the
    nearest graspable object becomes the grasp candidate that guards the
    pick transition. With no weights on disk the node logs one warning,
    publishes empty detections at rate and stays alive, so launch files
    and CI stay green. A target selector turns an operator's click in the
    3D panel into the mission target, validated against a range annulus and
    anchored in odom when the transform exists so the target does not drift
    while the robot moves, and fires the event that takes orbit into
    targeting. A health node grades every stream's rate onto diagnostics,
    and a missing publisher is a stale entry, never a crash.
  </p>

  <FortisPerceptionDiagram />

  <Clip id="visionDemo" />

  <h3>Visual odometry, and what happens when it fails</h3>

  <p>
    Visual odometry off the front camera is keyframe-anchored ORB matching
    with a ratio test, depth-lifted PnP with RANSAC and a refinement step,
    all in a pure module with no ROS imports. Every frame is matched
    against the current keyframe rather than the previous frame, so
    matching noise does not compound and drift accrues per keyframe. The
    node pairs each RGB frame with the newest cached depth under an
    explicit staleness bound instead of a strict synchronizer, because
    on-device compression latency wobbles and a strict pairing dropped
    whole frames. On tracking loss it publishes nothing, so the filter
    coasts on wheels and gyro instead of ingesting garbage, and keeps
    publishing the inlier count as a cheap health signal; its covariance
    scales with the inverse of that count so the filter's trust follows
    feature support.
  </p>

  <p>
    I validated the core two ways. Against the public TUM RGB-D handheld
    sequence with motion-capture ground truth, the integrated trajectory
    aligned rigidly to truth gives an absolute trajectory error of 0.052 m
    RMSE over an 8.01 m path, 0.7 percent, with zero tracking losses across
    all 792 frame pairs. Against the synthetic rig's exact orbit, the final
    error is bounded under 5 percent of path length as the acceptance test.
  </p>

  <h3>Wheel slip poisons the heading estimate</h3>

  <p>
    An Extended Kalman Filter fuses wheel odometry, visual odometry and the
    camera's inertial unit into one pose estimate, and the engineering is
    in what is excluded. Wheel odometry contributes translation only: the
    rear wheels slip under load and a slipping wheel injects a phantom yaw
    rate the gyroscope never sees, which makes wheel heading actively
    misleading. Visual odometry contributes translation only, because its
    rotation quality collapses with feature count while the gyro stays
    uniform. The gyro owns yaw. Neither source is fused as a pose, only as
    velocity, because both are open-loop dead reckoning and fusing their
    position would inject unbounded drift as though it were a measurement.
    The two filter configurations are held in lockstep by a test that
    asserts the visual variant is the base file plus one block.
  </p>

  <p>
    The gyro carries a zero-rate bias, and a stationary detector based on
    measured velocity could never fire, because bias-driven creep keeps the
    wheels turning while the drift being cancelled is active. The bias is
    estimated only while the drive is disarmed, when the motors are idle
    and the chassis is still by definition, into a slow moving average,
    with a settle window after each disarm because the chassis rocks as the
    motors release; while armed the estimate freezes and keeps subtracting.
    Wheel odometry reads joint velocities by name rather than index, since
    the broadcaster does not guarantee order, integrates exactly through
    the yaw turned over each interval rather than a zero-order hold, which
    removes the inward drift a Euler step accumulates while turning, and
    runs on message stamps rather than wall clock so it survives delayed,
    bursty or replayed input. Its twist covariance is deliberately loose,
    because the wheel velocity reaches the filter stale after the
    controller, the CAN frame and the control loop.
  </p>

  <h3>Testing a robot that did not exist yet</h3>

  <p>
    Unit tests run real in-process nodes over DDS and assert on what
    comes out of the wire, not internal state. Integration tests live in
    their own package for process and domain isolation, since launched
    nodes linger in DDS state and cross-talk with per-package helpers.
    They import topic names, QoS profiles and event identifiers from the
    production modules rather than hard-coding strings, because catching
    divergence between producer and consumer is the whole point. The
    acceptance test for the full mission brings up the synthetic camera,
    the perception chain, the state machine and the arm stack against the
    firmware mock and drives the happy path end to end: idle to orbit on the
    start event, orbit to targeting on a click that clears the range
    annulus, targeting to arm-at-view when the motion node reports the
    inverse kinematics solved and completes the move, into pick on a grasp
    candidate from detection, into holding, into return-home and back to
    idle, with every wait bounded and the whole run inside a 150 second
    budget.
  </p>

  <h3>Operator interface</h3>

  <p>
    The operator drives from Foxglove Studio over the tether, on the same
    ROS 2 graph the robot runs, with four saved layouts: teleop with the
    per-wheel velocity plot and the arm, disarm, stop and orbit buttons; the
    four chassis cameras with one streaming per tab; the perception view
    with clouds, map diff and detections; and the arm panel. The
    click-to-target flow is the mission interface: a click in the 3D panel
    becomes the target pose, the state machine advances, and the arm moves.
  </p>

  <Clip id="visionUi" />

  <h2>What ran on hardware</h2>

  <p>
    On the sponsor's mockup of the port, tunnel and step: the chassis
    traversed the full tunnel, sustained a toroidal strafe across the step
    above the 0.1 m/s target with zero tipping and zero wheel-lift events,
    the arm reached 32.7 inches and completed three of three pick-and-stow
    cycles with the payload retained, and the operator E-stop halted all
    motion in 1.58 s. The mockup is plastic rather than graphite, so it
    confirms fit and end-to-end control; the low-friction margin remains
    established by the simulation. Every wheel torque figure on this page
    traces to a sweep whose raw CSV output and plotting scripts are in the
    repository.
  </p>

